  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 122 AM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\n             -----------Synoptic Overview-----------\n\nA rather amplified upper-air pattern remains in place across the\nU.S. early this morning. An unseasonably cool trough sits over the\neastern Great Lakes, with additional jet stream energy passing\nclose to Glacier NP. Meanwhile, anomalous ridging extends from\nthe Chihuahuan Desert to the Greater Antilles. A weakness in the\nridge axis remains over Florida, where a weak shortwave trough\ncurrently resides.\n\nThrough at least Wednesday, the break in the ridge will remain very\nclose to Florida as additional weak ripples of vorticity reinforce\nthis general pattern. Then, from late week and into next weekend,\nglobal ensembles indicate that heights will build over the state as\nthe Bermuda high attempts to establish itself closer to the Bahamas.\n\nExpect the near-surface ridge axis to remain near or south of\nCentral Florida through at least Wednesday. Thereafter, surface high\npressure should become focused very close to or immediately north\nof the local area. PWAT (total moisture) values are forecast to\nsink slightly below normal early this week before climbing toward\nclimatological norms for the rest of the forecast period.\n\n             -------Sensible Weather & Impacts-------\n\nToday - Tuesday...\n\nThe overall setup will not change too much as we begin the work\nweek, aside from slightly drier air briefly filtering overhead. With\nbroad light offshore flow and an expected sea breeze each afternoon,\nat least scattered showers and storms are forecast. Chances range\nfrom 30-40% today up to 30-60% on Tuesday, with the highest chances\non Tuesday near and north of Orlando. Storms may linger into the\nevening due to late boundary collisions.\n\nWhile the drier air should limit overall coverage a bit, it may\nalso favor stronger downdrafts in storms that develop. There is\na low chance for wind gusts up to 50-55 mph.\n\nHigh temps will be hot, in the low to mid 90s each afternoon. Heat\nindices peak at 100 to 107.\n\nWednesday - Friday...\n\nAs high pressure nudges essentially overhead, the low-level sea\nbreeze circulation will be the dominant force for storm development\neach day. Light offshore flow in the mid-levels will try to steer\nshowers and storms back toward the coast in the evening hours. As\nmoisture returns to normal, this portends fairly high coverage of\nafternoon and early evening showers and storms for east central\nFlorida (50-70%).\n\nSeasonably hot conditions continue, with highs in the low to mid\n90s and heat indices of 100 to 107.\n\nNext Weekend...\n\nNear-normal moisture remains in place with high pressure\nstrengthening nearby or just northeast of the local area. Scattered\nafternoon and evening storms should continue, but the focus\nmay shift over the interior as mid-level offshore flow weakens. \nContinued seasonable heat and humidity.",
